# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Facility Issue Tracker — configuration
#
#   cp .env.example .env   and fill in the marked values.
#
# DEPLOY needs: DIGITALOCEAN_ACCESS_TOKEN, GITHUB_REPO, ADMIN_PASSWORD.
# Then `./scripts/link-local.sh > .env.local` to run the UI locally against
# the cluster you just deployed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Core ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=development

# Password for the /admin dashboard. Change it.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=complaints

# Signs the admin session cookie. Any long random string.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=dev-only-change-me

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DigitalOcean Managed Postgres, in every environment — there is no local
# database. deploy.sh binds this automatically on App Platform.
#
# To run the UI locally against the deployed cluster, don't set this by hand:
#
#   ./scripts/link-local.sh > .env.local
#
# which writes DATABASE_URL and the cluster CA into .env.local (it overrides
# .env). Until you have deployed once, there is nothing to point at.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host:25060/complaints?sslmode=require

# ── Harness Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every complaint fires this webhook, and a fresh microVM writes the ticket.
# There is no in-process alternative: the agent's instructions live in
# agents/complaint-prompt.txt and that is the only copy of them.
#
# Written for you by `./scripts/link-local.sh > .env.local` after a deploy.
# MARS_WEBHOOK_URL=
# MARS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# MARS_TICKET_TABLE=tickets

# ── DigitalOcean serverless inference ────────────────────────────────────────
# All model calls go through DigitalOcean's inference service, which speaks the
# Anthropic Messages API. Used by INGEST_MODE=local and by the MARS agent.
#
# INFERENCE_API_KEY: a DigitalOcean *model access key*. Required — deploy.sh
# refuses to run without one, and refuses one equal to your API token.
#
#   https://cloud.digitalocean.com/gen-ai/model-access-keys
#
# Not a personal access token. This key is handed to the intake agent as a
# secret, and that agent puts anonymous complaint text straight into its
# prompt while holding a shell and unrestricted egress. A model access key
# buys inference if it leaks. An account token buys the account.
INFERENCE_API_KEY=
INFERENCE_BASE_URL=https://inference.do-ai.run

# A DigitalOcean model slug, NOT an Anthropic model id.
#   doctl serverless-inference models list
# anthropic-claude-opus-5     best writing, ~4s per ticket
# anthropic-claude-haiku-4.5  ~3x faster, noticeably blander
INFERENCE_MODEL=qwen3.8-max

# ── GitHub issue tracker (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Each ticket also opens an issue in this repo, through Action Gateway.
#
# There is no token here on purpose. Action Gateway holds the GitHub
# credential and substitutes it when the tool runs, so it never reaches the
# sandbox. Authorize it once, in the control panel:
#
#   Managed Agents > Action Gateway > Connections > Add connection > GitHub
#
# With no connection the agent finds no tool, skips the issue, and still
# files the ticket. Leave GITHUB_ISSUE_REPO empty to drop the step entirely.
GITHUB_ISSUE_REPO=hgonzalez-do/facility_issue_tracker

# ── Deploy-time only (read by deploy.sh / destroy.sh, not by the app) ───────

# Your DigitalOcean API token. Needs read/write, and ALL scopes granted if you
# want it to double as the inference credential.
DIGITALOCEAN_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Where App Platform pulls the code from.
GITHUB_REPO=hgonzalez-do/facility_issue_demo
GITHUB_BRANCH=main

# Name prefix for every resource deploy.sh creates. Also what destroy.sh
# matches on, and what you type to confirm a teardown.
STACK_NAME=complaints-dept

# Datacentre for the app and the database. Must support Managed Postgres:
#   doctl databases options regions --engine pg
DO_REGION=nyc3

# Sizes. These are the cheapest that comfortably run the demo.
#   basic-xxs        $5/mo   512MB   the web tier does almost nothing
#   db-s-1vcpu-1gb   $15/mo  1GB     smallest single-node Postgres
# How many intake triggers to create. A Harness Runtime trigger runs exactly
# one session at a time, so this is how many complaints can be processed at
# once — one trigger means the nth person waits about n minutes. Triggers are
# free; only running sessions cost. Verified working at 8.
INTAKE_SHARDS=8

APP_SIZE=basic-xxs
DB_SIZE=db-s-1vcpu-1gb

# ── Reset, and the one credential the demo does not use ─────────────────────
#
# Optional. Leave it empty and everything works; Reset just leaves the tracker
# alone and you clear it by hand.
#
# Why it exists, stated plainly because a token in a config file deserves a
# reason. Everything the *demo* does with GitHub goes through Action Gateway,
# which holds the credential and substitutes it when a tool runs, so no token
# ever reaches a sandbox. That is the thing being demonstrated.
#
# Reset is not the demo. It is the button you press between rehearsals, and it
# needs one capability the demo deliberately lacks: delete. Action Gateway
# exposes update_issue and no delete — correct for an agent that should not be
# able to erase its own audit trail — so without this the tracker fills up with
# hundreds of closed issues over a conference day.
#
# Scope it tightly:
#   - a fine-grained PAT, this ONE repository, nothing else
#   - Issues: read & write, plus Metadata: read
#   - it needs admin on that repo, because GitHub's deleteIssue is a GraphQL
#     mutation that requires it; there is no REST delete and no lesser
#     permission that works
#   - give it an expiry and rotate it
#
# It is a SECRET in the App Platform spec, it is never passed to an agent, a
# trigger or a sandbox, and it is only reachable from POST /api/reset behind
# the admin password. See src/lib/github-admin.js.
GITHUB_ISSUE_TOKEN=
